        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The Ninth Circuit has adopted a treating physician rule in ERISA cases which requires the plan administrator to either accept the opinion of a treating physician or to reject that opinion by specific legitimate reasons based upon substantial evidence."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice O'Connor",
      "case_id": "2002-02-469",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c9baf0b805ab217e3adc61aefa38afb95e340433f73ce4cc1452a14673a953f9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c9baf0b805ab-0000-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: And is there a requirement to give reasons if there is a difference of views between the treating physician and the expert?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Lisa S. Blatt",
      "case_id": "2002-02-469",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c9baf0b805ab217e3adc61aefa38afb95e340433f73ce4cc1452a14673a953f9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c9baf0b805ab-0000-0003",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 18,
        "start": 18
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "No, Justice O'Connor. What the Secretary has done has -- is -- has not opposed -- imposed a treating physician requirement or otherwise constrained plan administrators in the way --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Blatt",
      "case_id": "2002-02-469",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c9baf0b805ab217e3adc61aefa38afb95e340433f73ce4cc1452a14673a953f9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c9baf0b805ab-0001-0000",
      "kind": "allegation",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 19,
        "start": 19
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "No. There's no evidence that they considered it."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Blatt",
      "case_id": "2002-02-469",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c9baf0b805ab217e3adc61aefa38afb95e340433f73ce4cc1452a14673a953f9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c9baf0b805ab-0001-0001",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 19,
        "start": 19
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "But they did overhaul their regulations for 2002 and did impose a lot of requirements, and they took a very different approach."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Blatt",
      "case_id": "2002-02-469",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c9baf0b805ab217e3adc61aefa38afb95e340433f73ce4cc1452a14673a953f9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c9baf0b805ab-0001-0002",
      "kind": "stated_consequence",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 23,
        "start": 23
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "But even if there was some inadequate explanation such that the court could not conduct meaningful judicial review, the standard consequence of that, Justice Scalia, is a remand back to the administrator for further explanation, not a de novo standard of review."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice",
      "case_id": "2002-02-469",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "c9baf0b805ab217e3adc61aefa38afb95e340433f73ce4cc1452a14673a953f9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c9baf0b805ab-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 36,
        "start": 36
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: Well, what is the connection between the treating physician rule and the concept of actual bias? The -- the two don't seem to have a lot in common so far as I can see."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Rohlfing",
      "case_id": "2002-02-469",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "c9baf0b805ab217e3adc61aefa38afb95e340433f73ce4cc1452a14673a953f9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c9baf0b805ab-0002-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 36,
        "start": 36
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. ROHLFING: The courts have created this hybrid animal that's asking whether conflict of interest exists and then using actual bias to -- to animate its -- its decisionmaking process. And that's the problem."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice",
      "case_id": "2002-02-469",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "c9baf0b805ab217e3adc61aefa38afb95e340433f73ce4cc1452a14673a953f9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c9baf0b805ab-0002-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 44,
        "start": 44
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: Whose money is at stake in the Social Security cases?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Rohlfing",
      "case_id": "2002-02-469",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "c9baf0b805ab217e3adc61aefa38afb95e340433f73ce4cc1452a14673a953f9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c9baf0b805ab-0002-0004",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 50,
        "start": 50
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "the -- the employer's statement that Ms. Forward had filled out before at the request of Metropolitan, actually supported the proposition of disability rather than supporting the proposition of no disability that had been advocated."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "American Medical Association",
      "case_id": "2002-02-469",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "c9baf0b805ab217e3adc61aefa38afb95e340433f73ce4cc1452a14673a953f9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c9baf0b805ab-0002-0006",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 52,
        "start": 52
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "In addition, the amicus, American Medical Association, has brought before this Court its statistics published in its own publication, the Journal of the American Medical Association. Those statistics state that 39 percent of treating physicians misrepresent symptoms, diagnosis, and severity of illness when their patients submit insurance claims."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Rehnquist",
      "case_id": "2002-02-469",
      "confidence": 0.8,
      "document_hash": "c9baf0b805ab217e3adc61aefa38afb95e340433f73ce4cc1452a14673a953f9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c9baf0b805ab-0003-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 53,
        "start": 53
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE REHNQUIST: Thank you, Mr. Paterson. The case is submitted."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2002-02-469",
      "confidence": 0.7,
      "document_hash": "c9baf0b805ab217e3adc61aefa38afb95e340433f73ce4cc1452a14673a953f9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c9baf0b805ab-0003-0003",
      "kind": "allegation",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 53,
        "start": 53
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "entitled to special weight, deference, and a presumption in ERISA disability benefit determinations when the professional organization of the treating physicians admits that treating physicians often make misrepresentations when their patients are filing insurance claims."
    }
  ],
